    {
      "number": 11,
      "section": "B",
      "marks": 17,
      "stem": "Let $f(x)=\\dfrac{2e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}$, $x\\in\\mathbb R$.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": "a",
          "prompt": "Find the coordinates of both intercepts of $y=f(x)$.",
          "marks": 3
        },
        {
          "label": "b",
          "prompt": "Show that $f$ is strictly increasing.",
          "marks": 4
        },
        {
          "label": "c",
          "prompt": "Find $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}f(x)$, justifying your answer.",
          "marks": 3
        },
        {
          "label": "d",
          "prompt": "Sketch $y=f(x)$, showing the intercepts and both horizontal asymptotes.",
          "marks": 3
        },
        {
          "label": "e",
          "prompt": "Explain why $f^{-1}$ exists, and find the exact value of $f^{-1}(1)$.",
          "marks": 4
        }
      ],
      "answerSummary": "Intercepts $(0,1/2)$ and $(-\\frac12\\ln2,0)$; asymptotes $y=-1,2$; $f^{-1}(1)=\\frac12\\ln2$.",
      "markScheme": [
        {
          "part": "a",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "The $y$-intercept is $(0,1/2)$."
            },
            {
              "code": "M1",
              "text": "For the $x$-intercept, $2e^{2x}-1=0$."
            },
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "The $x$-intercept is $(-\\frac12\\ln2,0)$."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "M1",
              "text": "Apply the quotient rule."
            },
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "$f\\prime(x)=[4e^{2x}(e^{2x}+1)-2e^{2x}(2e^{2x}-1)]/(e^{2x}+1)^2$."
            },
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "$f\\prime(x)=6e^{2x}/(e^{2x}+1)^2>0$."
            },
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "So $f$ is strictly increasing for every real $x$."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "c",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "M1",
              "text": "Divide numerator and denominator by $e^{2x}$."
            },
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "$f(x)=(2-e^{-2x})/(1+e^{-2x})$."
            },
            {
              "code": "R1",
              "text": "$e^{-2x}\\to0$, so the limit is $2$."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "d",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "Correct increasing S-shaped curve."
            },
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "Intercepts $(0,1/2)$ and $(-\\frac12\\ln2,0)$."
            },
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "Horizontal asymptotes $y=-1$ and $y=2$."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "e",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "R1",
              "text": "Strict monotonicity makes $f$ one-to-one."
            },
            {
              "code": "M1",
              "text": "Solve $f(x)=1$."
            },
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "$2e^{2x}-1=e^{2x}+1$, so $e^{2x}=2$."
            },
            {
              "code": "A1",
              "text": "$f^{-1}(1)=\\frac12\\ln2$."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sourcePages": [
        13,
        14
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 12,
      "section": "B",
      "marks": 20,
      "stem": "Let $f(x)=e^{x/5}$. A rectangle has vertices $(a,0)$, $(10,0)$, $(10,f(a))$ and $(a,f(a))$, where $0\\le a<10$. Its area is $S$ and perimeter is $P$.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": "a",
